  "body": "Found by the high-tier review of PR #1077 (review comment on that PR, head c6c658db). Present on main for part-sourced Bores; PR #1077 extends the same reading to stock-sourced Bore and Thread.\n\n**What happens.** Verify's rapid law (#96, `RapidInStock`) caps the stock column at the flat `stock_top_z`, the highest point of the whole stock. On a stock that is not a flat-topped box - a \"from model\" casting or a carried stock with a boss - a rapid down to just above a lower surface is judged as if the stock stood at its highest point there too.\n\nReviewer's probe: stock solid to Z 20 over the hole's mouth, with a boss to Z 30 elsewhere. A Bore on that hole (part-sourced and stock-sourced alike) rapids from clearance to Z 22, 2 mm above the real stock under it, and is convicted `RapidInStock { at: 0 }`. The post is blocked for a legal program.\n\n**Expected.** The law reads the stock's top at the rapid's XY (the local column), not the global maximum. A rapid that ends above the stock really under it passes; a rapid into stock that really stands there is still convicted.\n\n**Why P1.** It refuses, it does not gouge - but a user with a casting or a stepped stock cannot post a correct program. The probe also imported the casting as a model, which added a gouge refusal of its own, so the first step is a clean reproduction with stock only.\n\n**Careful.** This is a gouge law: loosening it must not let any rapid into real stock pass. Carried stock, a faced top (`acquit_by_prior`), the 3+2 site and Thread's descent all read the same cap.\n\nDone means: a pinning test with a stepped stock where the dive over the low part passes and the same dive over the boss is convicted, for a part-sourced and a stock-sourced Bore; the existing rapid-law tests (`ladder_entry_height`, the #96 pins) unchanged.\n",
